{"step_index":109,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:20Z","thinking":"**Examining potential pipelines**\n\nConsidering `pipeline/novelty.py` and `pipeline/linear_form.py` for relevant logic. Reviewing what the `pipeline` module has generated. The produced `Candidate` is a proposition defining a graph property.\n\n\n**Verifying candidate's validity**\n\nThe candidate conjecture `radius ≤ total_domination_number` originates from automated graph conjecturing. A literature theorem suggests that for connected graphs of order at least 2, total domination number is greater than or equal to the radius. Need to check if the graph class matches the theorem’s hypotheses and account for potential edge cases specific to the `GraphCalc` implementation.\n\nSpecifically, verify that the hypotheses of `GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass`, `GraphCalc.HasRadius`, and `GraphCalc.HasTotalDominationNumber` align with the theorem's preconditions: order at least 2, connectivity and no isolated vertices. The critical step is reviewing the precise definition of `IsTotalDominatingSet` within the framework.\n\n\n**Interpreting graph properties definitions**\n\nExamined the definition of `eccentricity`.  It calculates the smallest distance $k$ where all vertices are within $k$ hops of a given vertex `v`. This confirms the expected graph-theoretic interpretation of eccentricity. `radius G` is then determined as the minimum of the eccentricity values across all vertices in the graph. These definitions seem standard.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\nimport z3\n\ndef check_counterexample(n, target_rad, target_tdn):\n    # Search for a graph on n vertices with:\n    # 1. Connected\n    # 2. Total domination number <= target_tdn\n    # 3. Radius >= target_rad (i.e. every vertex has eccentricity >= target_rad)\n    s = z3.Solver()\n    \n    # Adj matrix\n    adj = [[z3.Bool(f\"e_{i}_{j}\") for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        s.add(z3.Not(adj[i][i]))\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            s.add(adj[i][j] == adj[j][i])\n    \n    # Has total domination number <= target_tdn:\n    # There exists a subset S of size <= target_tdn that is a total dominating set.\n    in_S = [z3.Bool(f\"in_S_{i}\") for i in range(n)]\n    # Sum in_S <= target_tdn\n    s.add(z3.AtMost(*in_S, target_tdn))\n    # Every vertex v has a neighbor in S:\n    for v in range(n):\n        # Or_{u} (adj[v][u] and in_S[u])\n        s.add(z3.Or([z3.And(adj[v][u], in_S[u]) for u in range(n)]))\n        \n    # Radius >= target_rad:\n    # For EVERY vertex v, eccentricity(v) >= target_rad.\n    # This means for every vertex v, there exists some vertex w such that dist(v, w) >= target_rad.\n    # Distances in graph: dist[i][j] in {0..n}\n    dist = [[z3.Int(f\"d_{i}_{j}\") for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            s.add(dist[i][j] >= 0, dist[i][j] <= n)\n            if i == j:\n                s.add(dist[i][j] == 0)\n            else:\n                # if adj[i][j], dist[i][j] == 1\n                # dist[i][j] <= dist[i][k] + 1 if adj[k][j]\n                s.add(z3.Implies(adj[i][j], dist[i][j] == 1))\n    \n    # Triangle inequality and shortest path consistency:\n    # for all i, j, k: dist[i][k] <= dist[i][j] + dist[j][k]\n    # To enforce exact distances:\n    # dist[i][j] == min_{k in N(j)} dist[i][k] + 1 for i != j\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i != j:\n                # dist[i][j] == 1 + min_{k in N(j)} dist[i][k]\n                # Each neighbor k gives dist[i][j] <= dist[i][k] + 1\n                for k in range(n):\n                    s.add(z3.Implies(adj[k][j], dist[i][j] <= dist[i][k] + 1))\n                # And dist[i][j] must be achieved by some neighbor:\n                s.add(z3.Or([z3.And(adj[k][j], dist[i][j] == dist[i][k] + 1) for k in range(n)]))\n    \n    # Connected: for all i, j, dist[i][j] < n (assuming n >= 2)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            s.add(dist[i][j] <= n - 1)\n            \n    # For every v, exists w such that dist[v][w] >= target_rad:\n    for v in range(n):\n        s.add(z3.Or([dist[v][w] >= target_rad for w in range(n)]))\n        \n    if s.check() == z3.sat:\n        print(f\"SAT for n={n}, rad>={target_rad}, tdn<={target_tdn}!\")\n        m = s.model()\n        edges = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            for j in range(i+1, n):\n                if z3.is_true(m.eval(adj[i][j])):\n                    edges.append((i, j))\n        print(\"Edges:\", edges)\n        return True\n    else:\n        print(f\"UNSAT for n={n}, rad>={target_rad}, tdn<={target_tdn}\")\n        return False\n\n# Test if radius >= 3 and tdn <= 2 for n=6..10\nfor n in range(4, 11):\n    if check_counterexample(n, 3, 2):\n        break\n'","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Running Z3 to test rad>=3 and tdn<=2","toolSummary":"Z3 test for rad>=3, tdn<=2"}}]}
