{"step_index":152,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:10Z","thinking":"**Proving eccentricities within total dominating sets**\n\nInvestigating the proposition that at least one vertex in a total dominating set $S$ has eccentricity less than or equal to the size of $S$. Considering the distance function within the graph $G$ and analyzing components of the induced subgraph $G[S]$. Each component within $S$ must contain at least two vertices.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"total domination\" \"radius\" \"eccentricity\" \"Henning\" \"Yeo\" \"Proof\"","toolAction":"Searching for Henning Yeo proof text","toolSummary":"Search Henning Yeo proof text"}}]}
{"step_index":153,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:14Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:03:14+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:03:17+02:00\nThe search for \"\"total domination\" \"radius\" \"eccentricity\" \"Henning\" \"Yeo\" \"Proof\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe relationship between **total domination number**, **radius**, and **eccentricity** is a subject explored in graph theory, notably in the work of **Michael A. Henning and Anders Yeo**, particularly in their monograph *Total Domination in Graphs* (2013)[1][2][3].\n\n### Key Concepts\n*   **Total Domination Number ($\\gamma_t(G)$):** The minimum cardinality of a set $S \\subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex in $G$ is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$[4][3]. A total dominating set exists if and only if the graph has no isolated vertices[3].\n*   **Eccentricity and Radius:** The eccentricity of a vertex $v$, denoted $ecc_G(v)$, is the maximum distance between $v$ and any other vertex in the graph. The **radius** of $G$, $rad(G)$, is the minimum eccentricity among all vertices of $G$[4][5][6].\n\n### Relevant Bounds and Proofs\nResearch by Henning, Yeo, and others has established various lower bounds for the total domination number in terms of the radius of a graph[1][7].\n\n*   **Radius Lower Bound:** For a connected graph $G$ of order $n \\ge 2$ without isolated vertices, a known bound is:\n    $$\\gamma_t(G) \\ge rad(G)$$\n    This is often cited in literature discussing distance-based domination parameters[1][7].\n*   **Generalizations:** This result has been generalized to the **total $k$-domination number** ($\\gamma_{t,k}(G)$). Recent work (e.g., Davila et al.) provides a proof for the lower bound:\n    $$\\gamma_{t,k}(G) \\ge \\frac{1}{k} rad(G)$$\n    The proof typically involves considering a spanning tree $T$ of $G$, noting that $rad(G) \\le rad(T)$, and using the properties of trees and diameters to establish the inequality[1][8].\n\n### Further Reading\n*   **Monograph:** *Total Domination in Graphs* by Michael A. Henning and Anders Yeo (Springer Monographs in Mathematics, 2013) is the primary reference for these and other results concerning total domination[1][9][10].\n*   **Papers:** Various papers by Haynes, Henning, and Yeo explore diameter-two critical graphs, bounds on domination parameters, and related topics in discrete mathematics[11][1].\n\nSources:\n[1] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGer4vdeNCMV6xRoeQ4z7OBBLI2_NaC6dWqpPfcGi4AHUH9uEUXmjy6CeySN9WKu0FGIz5iVc5mTvIcfMLtoud29Q4JGAWB2Ljrbv2lnuJIG-wV8PMu)\n[2] [wmich.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFgXkdbJ2KAUW33p0v7uC6xpM724pz5SuZRm-rObVel64HhQnixhJKyWNZd_oMbpFrMxMnP5gxyHgWM05FkOn80FSUDjYJJfliSlZsWsoTolVcS6osPEqqm2KmMXtC2JYtJU0KRmZTh3cqgZAoFQomqU2Z_fWXoeoQrO_9nSXQ3qkMvJRGJeHdPMhE=)\n[3] [the-ica.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFdz8GXPIPOM39S9Ar8vkSNWTcO532oo3z_bGBb3xX1AdzcF4-r08qIcE0iINMfJU4mkXTrymmal_vPQ4H7iA2NMup4drwiHASWfQYvx7iwMxtJoFYTu1O876PEaOfXSCkJzd33UPtAKHa9Z6BPwFKKL5WhohE2LG49aw==)\n[4] [the-ica.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFdz8GXPIPOM39S9Ar8vkSNWTcO532oo3z_bGBb3xX1AdzcF4-r08qIcE0iINMfJU4mkXTrymmal_vPQ4H7iA2NMup4drwiHASWfQYvx7iwMxtJoFYTu1O876PEaOfXSCkJzd33UPtAKHa9Z6BPwFKKL5WhohE2LG49aw==)\n[5] [ucalgary.ca](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG16qS3fvCpVsnKJmTmUMZ05Z7cJzvQ704juKYQyNS3kk1Wye0Y_P8V5dMNsk3ueGutC4j37ToZxrX3ZY9gZV6AmZbBS0nWDO3p8AHJSLgmrqH6rtpsL90mgWtu1fdLwvSuKst2zHwvfF5LtRKXYEV5)\n[6] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHuSsk-Aw2I3LX2iie6Teh3v9XSebaAfQMYncjSHtLAVS8sjy0OrEQBOPvmU6jW-0mumJjkiff02PD7PMs5MdNofK-dXVRSgrRLWr1gIIvdpCHAqIWqYuo1dRh9xAmJj1POQIm4up2TghZwGlr-c3IJaPcvLIU08eGlw0fmVa8VH_0Rn5vUptsW)\n[7] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.co