{"step_index":157,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:26Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:03:26+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:03:29+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Randy Davila\" \"total\" \"domination\" \"radius\" arxiv\" returned the following summary:\nThe research concerning \"total domination,\" \"radius,\" and Randy Davila can be found in the paper titled **\"Lower bounds for the total (distance) k-domination number of a graph,\"** which is available as an arXiv preprint[1][2][3].\n\n### Key Details:\n*   **Topic:** The paper explores lower bounds for the **total distance $k$-domination number** ($\\gamma_t^k(G)$) of a graph[1][3].\n*   **Radius Bound:** One of the specific contributions is the generalization of a known radius lower bound for the total domination number to the total $k$-domination number[1][2][3]. The authors state that for a connected graph $G$ of order $n \\ge 2$ and $k \\ge 1$:\n    $$\\gamma_t^k(G) \\ge \\frac{1}{k} \\text{rad}(G)$$\n*   **Context:** This work extends earlier results, such as those by DeLaViña et al., which established bounds for the total domination number (the $k=1$ case)[3].\n\nRandy Davila has authored or co-authored several papers on graph theory invariants, including other studies on distance domination and sub-$k$-domination numbers[4][5][6][7].\n\nSources:\n[1] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFrmv3fe9nb2ccnqc59V2XRvW8Vg2UlQcJMejwH2Lx6QamcSesZqYIiXRklURi579Qifuhk8ScHVLhACGAbUsabPT-wmdb2ytTxmkmAicwYEEUntx3dPw==)\n[2] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFrmv3fe9nb2ccnqc59V2XRvW8Vg2UlQcJMejwH2Lx6QamcSesZqYIiXRklURi579Qifuhk8ScHVLhACGAbUsabPT-wmdb2ytTxmkmAicwYEEUntx3dPw==)\n[3] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGKuiSGh0Yu-2_XnSESgd_vrbPT2oMTJi60SMGzI1uP9WKzOOlWFiURvPdL0i2-3EdBMAcOdjV25HK6AjchGrL4FG2YPb-heJ3lrH0SveGdK46SLa1Nly