{"step_index":163,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:43Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:03:43+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:03:43+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///tmp/agyhome.8pgH3g/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/b6952963-3663-455b-9c35-12fd88b926cb/.system_generated/steps/161/content.md`\nTotal Lines: 185\nTotal Bytes: 24910\nShowing lines 1 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: Title: Lower bounds for the total (distance) 𝑘-domination number of a graph\n2: \n3: Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ##### Report GitHub Issue\n8: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n9: [Learn more](https://info.arxiv.org/about)\n10: [Back to arXiv](https://arxiv.org/)\n11: [Why HTML?](https://info.arxiv.org/about/accessible_HTML.html)\n12: [Report Issue](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770)\n13: [Back to Abstract](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08770v1)\n14: [Download PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.08770v1)\n15: 1. [Abstract](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#abstract1)\n16: 2. [1 Introduction](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S1)\n17: 3. [2 Preliminaries](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S2)\n18: 4. [3 Lower Bounds for γkt​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G)](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S3)\n19: 5. [References](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#bib)\n20: [Abstract](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#abstract1)\n21: [1 Introduction](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S1)\n22: [2 Preliminaries](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S2)\n23: [3 Lower Bounds for γkt​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G)](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#S3)\n24: [References](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#bib)\n25: [License: CC BY 4.0](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n26: \n27: ###### Abstract\n28: For k≥1k\\geq 1 and a graph GG without isolated vertices, a total (distance) kk-dominating set of GG is a set of vertices S⊆V⁡(G)S\\subseteq V(G) such that every vertex in GG is within distance kk to some vertex of SS other than itself. The total (distance) kk-domination number of GG is the minimum cardinality of a total kk-dominating set in GG, and is denoted by γkt​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G). When k=1k=1, the total kk-domination number reduces to the total domination number, written γt​(G)\\gamma_{t}(G); that is, γt​(G)=γ1t​(G)\\gamma_{t}(G)=\\gamma_{1}^{t}(G). This paper shows that several known lower bounds on the total domination number generalize nicely to lower bounds on total (distance) kk-domination.\n29: Keywords: Total distance kk-domination; total domination. AMS subject classification: 05C69\n30: \n31: Distance domination in graphs is a well-known concept, with a recent survey estimating that there are more than 100 papers on the topic to date, including a chapter in the domination monograph by Haynes, Hedetniemi, and Henning [[6](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.0[8](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#bib.bib8)770#bib.bib6)]. This paper considers a specific, widely applicable variant of distance domination called total distance kk-domination, and from hereon, referred to as total kk-domination for simplicity. This concept was introduced by Henning, Oellermann, and Swart in [[9](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#bib.bib9)], where they define a total kk-dominating set of a graph GG to be a set S⊆V⁡(G)S\\subseteq V(G) of vertices so that every vertex is within distance kk from some vertex of SS other than itself. More specifically, let k≥1k\\geq 1 be an integer. A set S⊆V⁡(G)S\\subseteq V(G) is total kk-dominating in GG if for every vertex v∈V⁡(G)v\\in V(G), we have dG​(v,S∖{v})≤kd_{G}(v,S\\setminus\\{v\\})\\leq k. The total kk-domination number of GG is the cardinality of a minimum total kk-dominating set in GG and is denoted by γkt​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G). A total kk-dominating set of GG with cardinality γkt​(G)\\gamma_{k}^{t}(G) is called a γkt\\gamma_{k}^{t}-set of GG. Since every vertex vv of GG must be within distance kk to some vertex different from vv in a total kk-dominating set, we note that total kk-domination is not defined for any graph with isolated vertices. We further remark that when k=1k=1, total kk-domination reduces to a well-known and heavily studied notion of total domination. In particular, we note that γt​(G)=γ1t​(G)\\gamma_{t}(G)=\\gamma_{1}^{t}(G), where γt​(G)\\gamma_{t}(G) is the total domination number of GG. For more on total domination, see the excellent monograph by Henning and Yeo [8].\n32: As with the total domination number of a graph, the computation of the total kk-domination number is NP-hard [[6](https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08770#bib.bib6)]. For this reason, many of the results on the total kk-domination have focused primarily on finding tight upper and lower bounds. For example, the upper bound of Henning, Oellermann, and Swart [[9](https:/